---
title: SQLite Persistence
id: sqlite-persistence
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SQLite persistence stores Collection rows in a SQLite database supplied by the runtime. A new Collection can load those rows when it uses the same database. The wrapper can also save sync metadata so a supported sync adapter can resume safely."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose a package for your runtime. Each runtime package supplies a SQLite driver and re-exports "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"persistedCollectionOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/db-sqlite-persistence-core"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SQLite persistence stores Collection data. To retain mutations that still need a server response, use "},{"type":"link","href":"./offline-transactions.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"offline transactions"}]},{"type":"text","value":". The "},{"type":"link","href":"./offline-transactions.md#use-sqlite-persistence-with-the-outbox","children":[{"type":"text","value":"combined React Native recipe"}]},{"type":"text","value":" shows both stores together."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"choose-a-runtime-package","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose a runtime package"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each package connects through its runtime's database, storage, or IPC interface. Follow its README for setup and required native plugins."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Runtime"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Package and setup"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Factory"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Browser with OPFS"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/browser-db-sqlite-persistence#readme","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/browser-db-sqlite-persistence"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createBrowserWASQLitePersistence"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Node.js"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/node-db-sqlite-persistence#readme","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/node-db-sqlite-persistence"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createNodeSQLitePersistence"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"React Native with OP-SQLite"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/react-native-db-sqlite-persistence#readme","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/react-native-db-sqlite-persistence"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createReactNativeSQLitePersistence"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Expo with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"expo-sqlite"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/expo-db-sqlite-persistence#readme","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/expo-db-sqlite-persistence"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createExpoSQLitePersistence"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Capacitor"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/capacitor-db-sqlite-persistence#readme","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/capacitor-db-sqlite-persistence"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createCapacitorSQLitePersistence"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tauri"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/tauri-db-sqlite-persistence#readme","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/tauri-db-sqlite-persistence"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTauriSQLitePersistence"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Electron"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/electron-db-sqlite-persistence#readme","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/electron-db-sqlite-persistence"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createElectronSQLitePersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" in a renderer"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cloudflare Durable Objects (server)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/cloudflare-durable-objects-db-sqlite-persistence#readme","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/cloudflare-durable-objects-db-sqlite-persistence"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createCloudflareDOSQLitePersistence"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The core package has no SQLite engine binding. Use a runtime package unless you are implementing a new driver."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"start-in-the-browser","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start in the browser"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Install the browser wrapper and its SQLite engine:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"npm install @tanstack/db @tanstack/browser-db-sqlite-persistence @journeyapps/wa-sqlite","lang":"sh"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This example uses one browser tab. Open an OPFS database, then create one persistence instance for it. Give every persisted Collection a stable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"},{"type":"text","value":" and a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getKey"},{"type":"text","value":" function. Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"schemaVersion"},{"type":"text","value":" to track the stored row format. Increase it when that format changes."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createCollection } from '@tanstack/db'\nimport {\n  createBrowserWASQLitePersistence,\n  openBrowserWASQLiteOPFSDatabase,\n  persistedCollectionOptions,\n} from '@tanstack/browser-db-sqlite-persistence'\n\ntype Todo = {\n  id: string\n  title: string\n  completed: boolean\n}\n\nconst database = await openBrowserWASQLiteOPFSDatabase({\n  databaseName: 'app.sqlite',\n})\nconst persistence = createBrowserWASQLitePersistence({ database })\n\nconst todos = createCollection(\n  persistedCollectionOptions<Todo, string>({\n    id: 'todos',\n    getKey: (todo) => todo.id,\n    persistence,\n    schemaVersion: 1,\n  }),\n)\n\nawait todos.preload()\nconst transaction = todos.insert({\n  id: '1',\n  title: 'Buy milk',\n  completed: false,\n})\nawait transaction.when('settled')","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This Collection has no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sync"},{"type":"text","value":" option. Its normal insert, update, and delete handlers save local mutations to SQLite. It does not contact a server. The browser must support OPFS and run in a secure context. If multiple tabs can open the same database, use the "},{"type":"link","href":"#browser-tabs-and-electron-renderers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"multi-tab setup"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"manual-transactions-on-a-local-collection","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Manual transactions on a local Collection"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For a Collection without a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sync"},{"type":"text","value":" option, normal Collection mutations persist automatically. A manual transaction must call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"acceptMutations"},{"type":"text","value":" in its mutation function:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createTransaction } from '@tanstack/db'\n\nconst manualTransaction = createTransaction({\n  autoCommit: false,\n  mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    await todos.utils.acceptMutations(transaction)\n  },\n})\n\nmanualTransaction.mutate(() => {\n  todos.insert({ id: '2', title: 'Call home', completed: false })\n})\n\nawait manualTransaction.commit()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"load-rows-after-reopening-the-database","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Load rows after reopening the database"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep the database open while its Collections run. To simulate a page restart, clean up the Collection and close the database:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"await todos.cleanup()\nawait database.close?.()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Open the same OPFS database and create a Collection with the same "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"schemaVersion"},{"type":"text","value":". Load its rows before you read them:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const reopenedDatabase = await openBrowserWASQLiteOPFSDatabase({\n  databaseName: 'app.sqlite',\n})\nconst reopenedPersistence = createBrowserWASQLitePersistence({\n  database: reopenedDatabase,\n})\nconst reopenedTodos = createCollection(\n  persistedCollectionOptions<Todo, string>({\n    id: 'todos',\n    getKey: (todo) => todo.id,\n    persistence: reopenedPersistence,\n    schemaVersion: 1,\n  }),\n)\n\nawait reopenedTodos.preload()\nconsole.log(reopenedTodos.get('1')?.title) // 'Buy milk'\n\nawait reopenedTodos.cleanup()\nawait reopenedDatabase.close?.()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"temporal-values","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Temporal values"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SQLite persistence preserves "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Temporal.Instant"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Temporal.PlainDate"},{"type":"text","value":" as\nnative values, including nested fields, metadata and replay. Register the same\nTemporal implementation globally before writing or reopening persisted data.\nOn a runtime without native Temporal, for example:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import 'temporal-polyfill/global'","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Missing constructors and other Temporal kinds reject explicitly. Strings remain\nstrings. Equality retains the Temporal kind and canonical text, including a\nPlainDate's calendar; ordering follows Temporal comparison. Supported native\npredicates and persisted expression indexes use the existing SQL filtering and\nin-memory cleanup pipeline without reducing nanosecond precision."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This support applies to the shared SQLite adapter and local persisted wrapper.\nThe remote-subset coordinator wire domain does not include Temporal literals;\nthose requests reject before remote retry admission. If retained local demand\nbecomes remote after an ownership change, fallback sequence-gap recovery fails\nthe current Collection sync run before retrying that invalid request. This is a\nterminal Collection error: later active subsets are not recovered, subsequent\nloads reject with the same error, and later coordinator messages do not resume\nthe failed run. Existing acquisitions can still be released. Multiprocess\ntransport of native Temporal rows is not covered by this support claim."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Values already stored as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{}"},{"type":"text","value":" cannot be recovered. If an older version damaged\na synced cache, replace it through the existing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"schemaVersion"},{"type":"text","value":" and schema-reset\npolicy, then refetch. Updating the library does not automatically erase data.\nAlso rebuild an older cache if ordinary objects used the reserved\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"__tanstack_db_persisted_type__"},{"type":"text","value":" marker with a newly recognized type name. Older\nwrites did not escape those objects, so their original meaning is ambiguous.\nNew writes escape the marker and preserve the ordinary object.\nOlder library versions cannot interpret the new Temporal encoding. Changed\nexpression indexes rebuild when first ensured after upgrade. Later startups reuse\nthose indexes. A changed native-literal or escaped-record signature can leave an\nobsolete registry entry and index until an explicit schema reset; this release\ndoes not reclaim those entries automatically. Increment the affected synced\nCollection's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"schemaVersion"},{"type":"text","value":" and retain its reset policy to rebuild that cache.\nThe reset removes its old values and indexes before reading the new schema;\nother Collections with unchanged schema versions retain their caches. Refetch\nfrom the upstream source after reset."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"add-persistence-to-a-synced-collection","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Add persistence to a synced Collection"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass the options from a sync adapter into "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"persistedCollectionOptions"},{"type":"text","value":". The wrapper keeps that adapter's sync behavior and stores its applied data in SQLite."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For example, you can wrap a Query Collection. This separate example uses the imports and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Todo"},{"type":"text","value":" type from the browser example above:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { QueryClient } from '@tanstack/query-core'\nimport { queryCollectionOptions } from '@tanstack/query-db-collection'\n\nconst syncedDatabase = await openBrowserWASQLiteOPFSDatabase({\n  databaseName: 'synced.sqlite',\n})\nconst syncedPersistence = createBrowserWASQLitePersistence({\n  database: syncedDatabase,\n})\nconst queryClient = new QueryClient()\n\nconst syncedTodos = createCollection(\n  persistedCollectionOptions<Todo, string>({\n    ...queryCollectionOptions<Todo, string>({\n      id: 'synced-todos',\n      queryClient,\n      queryKey: ['todos'],\n      queryFn: async () => {\n        const response = await fetch('http://localhost:3000/api/todos')\n        if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Could not load todos')\n        return (await response.json()) as Array<Todo>\n      },\n      getKey: (todo) => todo.id,\n    }),\n    persistence: syncedPersistence,\n    schemaVersion: 1,\n    initialRender: {\n      strategy: 'network-first',\n      networkTimeoutMs: 3_000,\n    },\n  }),\n)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Install "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/query-core"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/query-db-collection"},{"type":"text","value":" to use this example. Change the URL to your server. You can wrap another Collection sync adapter in the same way."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialRender"},{"type":"text","value":" option is optional. It lets live queries report when SQLite restore completes. It also lets React and Solid Suspense use restored rows for the first render while the Collection is not ready."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"syncedDatabase"},{"type":"text","value":" open while "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"syncedTodos"},{"type":"text","value":" runs. During normal shutdown, await "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"syncedTodos.cleanup()"},{"type":"text","value":" and then "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"syncedDatabase.close?.()"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sync"},{"type":"text","value":" option determines which mode the wrapper uses:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Collection options"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SQLite behavior"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sync"},{"type":"text","value":" present"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Load persisted rows, then apply source sync transactions. The source remains responsible for current data."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sync"},{"type":"text","value":" absent"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Load and save local rows through a loopback sync adapter. The Collection has no remote source."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The wrapper also supports "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"syncMode: 'on-demand'"},{"type":"text","value":". In that mode, it loads rows for active query demand rather than loading every stored row into the Collection."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"render-after-sqlite-restore","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Render after SQLite restore"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialRender"},{"type":"text","value":" requires an eager persisted Collection. It does not support "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"syncMode: 'on-demand'"},{"type":"text","value":", which has no complete startup snapshot. Set it on every source Collection in a live query. If one source does not opt in, the query cannot report persisted readiness or use the restored snapshot as its initial-render fallback."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Install the React binding to use the following components:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"npm install @tanstack/react-db","lang":"sh"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A regular React live query exposes both readiness signals:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useLiveQuery } from '@tanstack/react-db'\n\nfunction TodosView() {\n  const result = useLiveQuery((q) => q.from({ syncedTodos }))\n  const readiness = result.isReady\n    ? 'Collection ready'\n    : result.isPersistedReady\n      ? 'SQLite restore complete. Collection readiness pending'\n      : 'Waiting for Collection or SQLite restore'\n\n  return (\n    <>\n      <p>{readiness}</p>\n      <ul>{result.data.map((todo) => <li key={todo.id}>{todo.title}</li>)}</ul>\n    </>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isReady"},{"type":"text","value":" reports Collection readiness. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isPersistedReady"},{"type":"text","value":" reports that every opted-in source finished its current eager restore, even when SQLite had no rows. These flags do not identify where each visible row came from. A regular "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLiveQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" does not wait for the network-first deadline before it renders rows."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The hooks also expose "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"persistedStatus"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"persistedError"},{"type":"text","value":". The status is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"unavailable"},{"type":"text","value":" if a source did not opt in, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"loading"},{"type":"text","value":" during restore, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ready"},{"type":"text","value":" after every restore completes, or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"error"},{"type":"text","value":" if a restore fails. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"persistedError"},{"type":"text","value":" keeps the restore error. An SSR seed does not count as a SQLite restore."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"React, Solid, and Svelte read these values as properties. Angular reads signals such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"result.isPersistedReady()"},{"type":"text","value":". Vue reads refs such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"result.isPersistedReady.value"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"React and Solid Suspense can use the same option for network-first initial rendering. This React component uses the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"syncedTodos"},{"type":"text","value":" Collection configured above:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { Suspense } from 'react'\nimport { useLiveSuspenseQuery } from '@tanstack/react-db'\n\nfunction TodosList() {\n  const { data } = useLiveSuspenseQuery((q) => q.from({ syncedTodos }))\n  return <ul>{data.map((todo) => <li key={todo.id}>{todo.title}</li>)}</ul>\n}\n\nfunction TodosScreen() {\n  return (\n    <Suspense fallback={<p>Loading todos...</p>}>\n      <TodosList />\n    </Suspense>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Place "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TodosScreen"},{"type":"text","value":" inside an "},{"type":"link","href":"./error-handling.md#using-suspense-and-error-boundaries-react","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Error Boundary"}]},{"type":"text","value":" to handle initial failures."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Suspense prefers Collection readiness. If it remains pending after "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"networkTimeoutMs"},{"type":"text","value":", a completed SQLite restore can release the first render. The default is 3 seconds. Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"networkTimeoutMs: 0"},{"type":"text","value":" to allow fallback as soon as restore completes. For a query with multiple sources, the longest configured deadline applies. The deadline does not stop network sync."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A source failure can allow fallback before the deadline. React also waits for restore if its client query stream fails before the first render, including before the component mounts. Once every restore succeeds, React renders the derived query result, even if it is empty."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A derived-query failure still reaches the surrounding React Error Boundary. If the network-first wait rejects, its error reaches that boundary. A successful network load can render even if restore failed or a source did not opt in."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Persisted readiness does not prove current authorization. Isolate SQLite data by user or tenant. If an authorization failure must hide cached rows, block the view separately. The fallback does not classify source errors."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"browser-tabs-and-electron-renderers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Browser tabs and Electron renderers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The browser package uses single-tab coordination by default. If tabs share one OPFS database, give the persistence instance a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BrowserCollectionCoordinator"},{"type":"text","value":". It elects an owner for each Collection and routes writes between tabs. Replace the initial database and persistence setup with this code in each tab:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { BrowserCollectionCoordinator } from '@tanstack/browser-db-sqlite-persistence'\n\nconst database = await openBrowserWASQLiteOPFSDatabase({\n  databaseName: 'app.sqlite',\n})\nconst coordinator = new BrowserCollectionCoordinator({ dbName: 'app' })\nconst persistence = createBrowserWASQLitePersistence({\n  database,\n  coordinator,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass this persistence instance to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"persistedCollectionOptions"},{"type":"text","value":". During normal shutdown, clean up the Collections, dispose the coordinator, and close the database."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Electron package uses a main-process SQLite owner and a renderer bridge. Add "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ElectronCollectionCoordinator"},{"type":"text","value":" when multiple renderers share the database."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"These coordinators belong to SQLite persistence. The offline transaction executor has separate leader election for its outbox. Configure each system for the storage it owns."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The browser database uses a worker. Its OPFS setup needs a secure context and browser support for OPFS. Close the database during normal shutdown. After a page returns from the back/forward cache, create fresh database and Collection instances."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"node-js-alternative","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Node.js alternative"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For a Node.js process, install "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/node-db-sqlite-persistence"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"better-sqlite3"},{"type":"text","value":" in place of the browser packages. Open a file with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"better-sqlite3"},{"type":"text","value":" and pass it to the Node factory:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import Database from 'better-sqlite3'\nimport {\n  createNodeSQLitePersistence,\n  persistedCollectionOptions,\n} from '@tanstack/node-db-sqlite-persistence'\n\nconst nodeDatabase = new Database('./app.sqlite')\nconst nodePersistence = createNodeSQLitePersistence({\n  database: nodeDatabase,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"nodePersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" in the Collection options shown above. Clean up each Collection before you call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"nodeDatabase.close()"},{"type":"text","value":". The "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/node-db-sqlite-persistence#readme","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Node package README"}]},{"type":"text","value":" has a complete example."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"schema-versions-and-recovery","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Schema versions and recovery"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, a synced Collection resets its persisted data after a schema mismatch. Its sync adapter must then load current data. A Collection without "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sync"},{"type":"text","value":" reports an error instead. It has no source that can restore deleted local rows."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"All runtime factories listed above except the Electron renderer factory accept "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"schemaMismatchPolicy"},{"type":"text","value":". An explicit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reset"},{"type":"text","value":" policy can delete local data, so use it only when your application can restore that data."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Node package prunes its applied transaction log by default. Its "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/node-db-sqlite-persistence#applied-transaction-pruning","children":[{"type":"text","value":"README"}]},{"type":"text","value":" lists the limits and options. If a resume position is older than the retained log, persistence loads a full snapshot."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"errors-and-limits","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Errors and limits"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An unavailable browser storage capability raises "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PersistenceUnavailableError"},{"type":"text","value":". A durable write failure raises "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PersistedCollectionDurabilityError"},{"type":"text","value":" and puts the Collection in its error state."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If a coordinated write loses its response, the result can be uncertain. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"IndeterminateCommitError"},{"type":"text","value":" means the application must check the durable state before it retries that write."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Persistence retains source data and metadata. It does not define how an application sends local changes to a server. Use a mutation handler or the "},{"type":"link","href":"./offline-transactions.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"offline transactions guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for that work."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"choose-a-runtime-package","text":"Choose a runtime package","level":2},{"id":"start-in-the-browser","text":"Start in the browser","level":2},{"id":"manual-transactions-on-a-local-collection","text":"Manual transactions on a local Collection","level":3},{"id":"load-rows-after-reopening-the-database","text":"Load rows after reopening the database","level":3},{"id":"temporal-values","text":"Temporal values","level":2},{"id":"add-persistence-to-a-synced-collection","text":"Add persistence to a synced Collection","level":2},{"id":"render-after-sqlite-restore","text":"Render after SQLite restore","level":2},{"id":"browser-tabs-and-electron-renderers","text":"Browser tabs and Electron renderers","level":2},{"id":"node-js-alternative","text":"Node.js alternative","level":2},{"id":"schema-versions-and-recovery","text":"Schema versions and recovery","level":2},{"id":"errors-and-limits","text":"Errors and limits","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nSQLite persistence stores Collection rows in a SQLite database supplied by the runtime. A new Collection can load those rows when it uses the same database. The wrapper can also save sync metadata so a supported sync adapter can resume safely.\n\nChoose a package for your runtime. Each runtime package supplies a SQLite driver and re-exports `persistedCollectionOptions` from `@tanstack/db-sqlite-persistence-core`.\n\nSQLite persistence stores Collection data. To retain mutations that still need a server response, use [offline transactions](./offline-transactions.md). The [combined React Native recipe](./offline-transactions.md#use-sqlite-persistence-with-the-outbox) shows both stores together.\n\n## Choose a runtime package\n\nEach package connects through its runtime's database, storage, or IPC interface. Follow its README for setup and required native plugins.\n\n| Runtime | Package and setup | Factory |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Browser with OPFS | [`@tanstack/browser-db-sqlite-persistence`](https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/browser-db-sqlite-persistence#readme) | `createBrowserWASQLitePersistence` |\n| Node.js | [`@tanstack/node-db-sqlite-persistence`](https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/node-db-sqlite-persistence#readme) | `createNodeSQLitePersistence` |\n| React Native with OP-SQLite | [`@tanstack/react-native-db-sqlite-persistence`](https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/react-native-db-sqlite-persistence#readme) | `createReactNativeSQLitePersistence` |\n| Expo with `expo-sqlite` | [`@tanstack/expo-db-sqlite-persistence`](https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/expo-db-sqlite-persistence#readme) | `createExpoSQLitePersistence` |\n| Capacitor | [`@tanstack/capacitor-db-sqlite-persistence`](https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/capacitor-db-sqlite-persistence#readme) | `createCapacitorSQLitePersistence` |\n| Tauri | [`@tanstack/tauri-db-sqlite-persistence`](https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/tauri-db-sqlite-persistence#readme) | `createTauriSQLitePersistence` |\n| Electron | [`@tanstack/electron-db-sqlite-persistence`](https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/electron-db-sqlite-persistence#readme) | `createElectronSQLitePersistence` in a renderer |\n| Cloudflare Durable Objects (server) | [`@tanstack/cloudflare-durable-objects-db-sqlite-persistence`](https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/cloudflare-durable-objects-db-sqlite-persistence#readme) | `createCloudflareDOSQLitePersistence` |\n\nThe core package has no SQLite engine binding. Use a runtime package unless you are implementing a new driver.\n\n## Start in the browser\n\nInstall the browser wrapper and its SQLite engine:\n\n```sh\nnpm install @tanstack/db @tanstack/browser-db-sqlite-persistence @journeyapps/wa-sqlite\n```\n\nThis example uses one browser tab. Open an OPFS database, then create one persistence instance for it. Give every persisted Collection a stable `id` and a `getKey` function. Set `schemaVersion` to track the stored row format. Increase it when that format changes.\n\n```ts\nimport { createCollection } from '@tanstack/db'\nimport {\n  createBrowserWASQLitePersistence,\n  openBrowserWASQLiteOPFSDatabase,\n  persistedCollectionOptions,\n} from '@tanstack/browser-db-sqlite-persistence'\n\ntype Todo = {\n  id: string\n  title: string\n  completed: boolean\n}\n\nconst database = await openBrowserWASQLiteOPFSDatabase({\n  databaseName: 'app.sqlite',\n})\nconst persistence = createBrowserWASQLitePersistence({ database })\n\nconst todos = createCollection(\n  persistedCollectionOptions<Todo, string>({\n    id: 'todos',\n    getKey: (todo) => todo.id,\n    persistence,\n    schemaVersion: 1,\n  }),\n)\n\nawait todos.preload()\nconst transaction = todos.insert({\n  id: '1',\n  title: 'Buy milk',\n  completed: false,\n})\nawait transaction.when('settled')\n```\n\nThis Collection has no `sync` option. Its normal insert, update, and delete handlers save local mutations to SQLite. It does not contact a server. The browser must support OPFS and run in a secure context. If multiple tabs can open the same database, use the [multi-tab setup](#browser-tabs-and-electron-renderers).\n\n### Manual transactions on a local Collection\n\nFor a Collection without a `sync` option, normal Collection mutations persist automatically. A manual transaction must call `acceptMutations` in its mutation function:\n\n```ts\nimport { createTransaction } from '@tanstack/db'\n\nconst manualTransaction = createTransaction({\n  autoCommit: false,\n  mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    await todos.utils.acceptMutations(transaction)\n  },\n})\n\nmanualTransaction.mutate(() => {\n  todos.insert({ id: '2', title: 'Call home', completed: false })\n})\n\nawait manualTransaction.commit()\n```\n\n### Load rows after reopening the database\n\nKeep the database open while its Collections run. To simulate a page restart, clean up the Collection and close the database:\n\n```ts\nawait todos.cleanup()\nawait database.close?.()\n```\n\nOpen the same OPFS database and create a Collection with the same `id` and `schemaVersion`. Load its rows before you read them:\n\n```ts\nconst reopenedDatabase = await openBrowserWASQLiteOPFSDatabase({\n  databaseName: 'app.sqlite',\n})\nconst reopenedPersistence = createBrowserWASQLitePersistence({\n  database: reopenedDatabase,\n})\nconst reopenedTodos = createCollection(\n  persistedCollectionOptions<Todo, string>({\n    id: 'todos',\n    getKey: (todo) => todo.id,\n    persistence: reopenedPersistence,\n    schemaVersion: 1,\n  }),\n)\n\nawait reopenedTodos.preload()\nconsole.log(reopenedTodos.get('1')?.title) // 'Buy milk'\n\nawait reopenedTodos.cleanup()\nawait reopenedDatabase.close?.()\n```\n\n## Temporal values\n\nSQLite persistence preserves `Temporal.Instant` and `Temporal.PlainDate` as\nnative values, including nested fields, metadata and replay. Register the same\nTemporal implementation globally before writing or reopening persisted data.\nOn a runtime without native Temporal, for example:\n\n```ts\nimport 'temporal-polyfill/global'\n```\n\nMissing constructors and other Temporal kinds reject explicitly. Strings remain\nstrings. Equality retains the Temporal kind and canonical text, including a\nPlainDate's calendar; ordering follows Temporal comparison. Supported native\npredicates and persisted expression indexes use the existing SQL filtering and\nin-memory cleanup pipeline without reducing nanosecond precision.\n\nThis support applies to the shared SQLite adapter and local persisted wrapper.\nThe remote-subset coordinator wire domain does not include Temporal literals;\nthose requests reject before remote retry admission. If retained local demand\nbecomes remote after an ownership change, fallback sequence-gap recovery fails\nthe current Collection sync run before retrying that invalid request. This is a\nterminal Collection error: later active subsets are not recovered, subsequent\nloads reject with the same error, and later coordinator messages do not resume\nthe failed run. Existing acquisitions can still be released. Multiprocess\ntransport of native Temporal rows is not covered by this support claim.\n\nValues already stored as `{}` cannot be recovered. If an older version damaged\na synced cache, replace it through the existing `schemaVersion` and schema-reset\npolicy, then refetch. Updating the library does not automatically erase data.\nAlso rebuild an older cache if ordinary objects used the reserved\n`__tanstack_db_persisted_type__` marker with a newly recognized type name. Older\nwrites did not escape those objects, so their original meaning is ambiguous.\nNew writes escape the marker and preserve the ordinary object.\nOlder library versions cannot interpret the new Temporal encoding. Changed\nexpression indexes rebuild when first ensured after upgrade. Later startups reuse\nthose indexes. A changed native-literal or escaped-record signature can leave an\nobsolete registry entry and index until an explicit schema reset; this release\ndoes not reclaim those entries automatically. Increment the affected synced\nCollection's `schemaVersion` and retain its reset policy to rebuild that cache.\nThe reset removes its old values and indexes before reading the new schema;\nother Collections with unchanged schema versions retain their caches. Refetch\nfrom the upstream source after reset.\n\n## Add persistence to a synced Collection\n\nPass the options from a sync adapter into `persistedCollectionOptions`. The wrapper keeps that adapter's sync behavior and stores its applied data in SQLite.\n\nFor example, you can wrap a Query Collection. This separate example uses the imports and `Todo` type from the browser example above:\n\n```ts\nimport { QueryClient } from '@tanstack/query-core'\nimport { queryCollectionOptions } from '@tanstack/query-db-collection'\n\nconst syncedDatabase = await openBrowserWASQLiteOPFSDatabase({\n  databaseName: 'synced.sqlite',\n})\nconst syncedPersistence = createBrowserWASQLitePersistence({\n  database: syncedDatabase,\n})\nconst queryClient = new QueryClient()\n\nconst syncedTodos = createCollection(\n  persistedCollectionOptions<Todo, string>({\n    ...queryCollectionOptions<Todo, string>({\n      id: 'synced-todos',\n      queryClient,\n      queryKey: ['todos'],\n      queryFn: async () => {\n        const response = await fetch('http://localhost:3000/api/todos')\n        if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Could not load todos')\n        return (await response.json()) as Array<Todo>\n      },\n      getKey: (todo) => todo.id,\n    }),\n    persistence: syncedPersistence,\n    schemaVersion: 1,\n    initialRender: {\n      strategy: 'network-first',\n      networkTimeoutMs: 3_000,\n    },\n  }),\n)\n```\n\nInstall `@tanstack/query-core` and `@tanstack/query-db-collection` to use this example. Change the URL to your server. You can wrap another Collection sync adapter in the same way.\n\nThe `initialRender` option is optional. It lets live queries report when SQLite restore completes. It also lets React and Solid Suspense use restored rows for the first render while the Collection is not ready.\n\nKeep `syncedDatabase` open while `syncedTodos` runs. During normal shutdown, await `syncedTodos.cleanup()` and then `syncedDatabase.close?.()`.\n\nThe `sync` option determines which mode the wrapper uses:\n\n| Collection options | SQLite behavior |\n| --- | --- |\n| `sync` present | Load persisted rows, then apply source sync transactions. The source remains responsible for current data. |\n| `sync` absent | Load and save local rows through a loopback sync adapter. The Collection has no remote source. |\n\nThe wrapper also supports `syncMode: 'on-demand'`. In that mode, it loads rows for active query demand rather than loading every stored row into the Collection.\n\n## Render after SQLite restore\n\n`initialRender` requires an eager persisted Collection. It does not support `syncMode: 'on-demand'`, which has no complete startup snapshot. Set it on every source Collection in a live query. If one source does not opt in, the query cannot report persisted readiness or use the restored snapshot as its initial-render fallback.\n\nInstall the React binding to use the following components:\n\n```sh\nnpm install @tanstack/react-db\n```\n\nA regular React live query exposes both readiness signals:\n\n```tsx\nimport { useLiveQuery } from '@tanstack/react-db'\n\nfunction TodosView() {\n  const result = useLiveQuery((q) => q.from({ syncedTodos }))\n  const readiness = result.isReady\n    ? 'Collection ready'\n    : result.isPersistedReady\n      ? 'SQLite restore complete. Collection readiness pending'\n      : 'Waiting for Collection or SQLite restore'\n\n  return (\n    <>\n      <p>{readiness}</p>\n      <ul>{result.data.map((todo) => <li key={todo.id}>{todo.title}</li>)}</ul>\n    </>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n`isReady` reports Collection readiness. `isPersistedReady` reports that every opted-in source finished its current eager restore, even when SQLite had no rows. These flags do not identify where each visible row came from. A regular `useLiveQuery` does not wait for the network-first deadline before it renders rows.\n\nThe hooks also expose `persistedStatus` and `persistedError`. The status is `unavailable` if a source did not opt in, `loading` during restore, `ready` after every restore completes, or `error` if a restore fails. `persistedError` keeps the restore error. An SSR seed does not count as a SQLite restore.\n\nReact, Solid, and Svelte read these values as properties. Angular reads signals such as `result.isPersistedReady()`. Vue reads refs such as `result.isPersistedReady.value`.\n\nReact and Solid Suspense can use the same option for network-first initial rendering. This React component uses the `syncedTodos` Collection configured above:\n\n```tsx\nimport { Suspense } from 'react'\nimport { useLiveSuspenseQuery } from '@tanstack/react-db'\n\nfunction TodosList() {\n  const { data } = useLiveSuspenseQuery((q) => q.from({ syncedTodos }))\n  return <ul>{data.map((todo) => <li key={todo.id}>{todo.title}</li>)}</ul>\n}\n\nfunction TodosScreen() {\n  return (\n    <Suspense fallback={<p>Loading todos...</p>}>\n      <TodosList />\n    </Suspense>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nPlace `TodosScreen` inside an [Error Boundary](./error-handling.md#using-suspense-and-error-boundaries-react) to handle initial failures.\n\nSuspense prefers Collection readiness. If it remains pending after `networkTimeoutMs`, a completed SQLite restore can release the first render. The default is 3 seconds. Set `networkTimeoutMs: 0` to allow fallback as soon as restore completes. For a query with multiple sources, the longest configured deadline applies. The deadline does not stop network sync.\n\nA source failure can allow fallback before the deadline. React also waits for restore if its client query stream fails before the first render, including before the component mounts. Once every restore succeeds, React renders the derived query result, even if it is empty.\n\nA derived-query failure still reaches the surrounding React Error Boundary. If the network-first wait rejects, its error reaches that boundary. A successful network load can render even if restore failed or a source did not opt in.\n\nPersisted readiness does not prove current authorization. Isolate SQLite data by user or tenant. If an authorization failure must hide cached rows, block the view separately. The fallback does not classify source errors.\n\n## Browser tabs and Electron renderers\n\nThe browser package uses single-tab coordination by default. If tabs share one OPFS database, give the persistence instance a `BrowserCollectionCoordinator`. It elects an owner for each Collection and routes writes between tabs. Replace the initial database and persistence setup with this code in each tab:\n\n```ts\nimport { BrowserCollectionCoordinator } from '@tanstack/browser-db-sqlite-persistence'\n\nconst database = await openBrowserWASQLiteOPFSDatabase({\n  databaseName: 'app.sqlite',\n})\nconst coordinator = new BrowserCollectionCoordinator({ dbName: 'app' })\nconst persistence = createBrowserWASQLitePersistence({\n  database,\n  coordinator,\n})\n```\n\nPass this persistence instance to `persistedCollectionOptions`. During normal shutdown, clean up the Collections, dispose the coordinator, and close the database.\n\nThe Electron package uses a main-process SQLite owner and a renderer bridge. Add `ElectronCollectionCoordinator` when multiple renderers share the database.\n\nThese coordinators belong to SQLite persistence. The offline transaction executor has separate leader election for its outbox. Configure each system for the storage it owns.\n\nThe browser database uses a worker. Its OPFS setup needs a secure context and browser support for OPFS. Close the database during normal shutdown. After a page returns from the back/forward cache, create fresh database and Collection instances.\n\n## Node.js alternative\n\nFor a Node.js process, install `@tanstack/node-db-sqlite-persistence` and `better-sqlite3` in place of the browser packages. Open a file with `better-sqlite3` and pass it to the Node factory:\n\n```ts\nimport Database from 'better-sqlite3'\nimport {\n  createNodeSQLitePersistence,\n  persistedCollectionOptions,\n} from '@tanstack/node-db-sqlite-persistence'\n\nconst nodeDatabase = new Database('./app.sqlite')\nconst nodePersistence = createNodeSQLitePersistence({\n  database: nodeDatabase,\n})\n```\n\nUse `nodePersistence` in the Collection options shown above. Clean up each Collection before you call `nodeDatabase.close()`. The [Node package README](https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/node-db-sqlite-persistence#readme) has a complete example.\n\n## Schema versions and recovery\n\nBy default, a synced Collection resets its persisted data after a schema mismatch. Its sync adapter must then load current data. A Collection without `sync` reports an error instead. It has no source that can restore deleted local rows.\n\nAll runtime factories listed above except the Electron renderer factory accept `schemaMismatchPolicy`. An explicit `reset` policy can delete local data, so use it only when your application can restore that data.\n\nThe Node package prunes its applied transaction log by default. Its [README](https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/node-db-sqlite-persistence#applied-transaction-pruning) lists the limits and options. If a resume position is older than the retained log, persistence loads a full snapshot.\n\n## Errors and limits\n\nAn unavailable browser storage capability raises `PersistenceUnavailableError`. A durable write failure raises `PersistedCollectionDurabilityError` and puts the Collection in its error state.\n\nIf a coordinated write loses its response, the result can be uncertain. `IndeterminateCommitError` means the application must check the durable state before it retries that write.\n\nPersistence retains source data and metadata. It does not define how an application sends local changes to a server. Use a mutation handler or the [offline transactions guide](./offline-transactions.md) for that work.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: SQLite Persistence\nid: sqlite-persistence\n---\n\nSQLite persistence stores Collection rows in a SQLite database supplied by the runtime. A new Collection can load those rows when it uses the same database. The wrapper can also save sync metadata so a supported sync adapter can resume safely.\n\nChoose a package for your runtime. Each runtime package supplies a SQLite driver and re-exports `persistedCollectionOptions` from `@tanstack/db-sqlite-persistence-core`.\n\nSQLite persistence stores Collection data. To retain mutations that still need a server response, use [offline transactions](./offline-transactions.md). The [combined React Native recipe](./offline-transactions.md#use-sqlite-persistence-with-the-outbox) shows both stores together.\n\n## Choose a runtime package\n\nEach package connects through its runtime's database, storage, or IPC interface. Follow its README for setup and required native plugins.\n\n| Runtime | Package and setup | Factory |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Browser with OPFS | [`@tanstack/browser-db-sqlite-persistence`](https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/browser-db-sqlite-persistence#readme) | `createBrowserWASQLitePersistence` |\n| Node.js | [`@tanstack/node-db-sqlite-persistence`](https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/node-db-sqlite-persistence#readme) | `createNodeSQLitePersistence` |\n| React Native with OP-SQLite | [`@tanstack/react-native-db-sqlite-persistence`](https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/react-native-db-sqlite-persistence#readme) | `createReactNativeSQLitePersistence` |\n| Expo with `expo-sqlite` | [`@tanstack/expo-db-sqlite-persistence`](https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/expo-db-sqlite-persistence#readme) | `createExpoSQLitePersistence` |\n| Capacitor | [`@tanstack/capacitor-db-sqlite-persistence`](https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/capacitor-db-sqlite-persistence#readme) | `createCapacitorSQLitePersistence` |\n| Tauri | [`@tanstack/tauri-db-sqlite-persistence`](https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/tauri-db-sqlite-persistence#readme) | `createTauriSQLitePersistence` |\n| Electron | [`@tanstack/electron-db-sqlite-persistence`](https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/electron-db-sqlite-persistence#readme) | `createElectronSQLitePersistence` in a renderer |\n| Cloudflare Durable Objects (server) | [`@tanstack/cloudflare-durable-objects-db-sqlite-persistence`](https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/cloudflare-durable-objects-db-sqlite-persistence#readme) | `createCloudflareDOSQLitePersistence` |\n\nThe core package has no SQLite engine binding. Use a runtime package unless you are implementing a new driver.\n\n## Start in the browser\n\nInstall the browser wrapper and its SQLite engine:\n\n```sh\nnpm install @tanstack/db @tanstack/browser-db-sqlite-persistence @journeyapps/wa-sqlite\n```\n\nThis example uses one browser tab. Open an OPFS database, then create one persistence instance for it. Give every persisted Collection a stable `id` and a `getKey` function. Set `schemaVersion` to track the stored row format. Increase it when that format changes.\n\n```ts\nimport { createCollection } from '@tanstack/db'\nimport {\n  createBrowserWASQLitePersistence,\n  openBrowserWASQLiteOPFSDatabase,\n  persistedCollectionOptions,\n} from '@tanstack/browser-db-sqlite-persistence'\n\ntype Todo = {\n  id: string\n  title: string\n  completed: boolean\n}\n\nconst database = await openBrowserWASQLiteOPFSDatabase({\n  databaseName: 'app.sqlite',\n})\nconst persistence = createBrowserWASQLitePersistence({ database })\n\nconst todos = createCollection(\n  persistedCollectionOptions<Todo, string>({\n    id: 'todos',\n    getKey: (todo) => todo.id,\n    persistence,\n    schemaVersion: 1,\n  }),\n)\n\nawait todos.preload()\nconst transaction = todos.insert({\n  id: '1',\n  title: 'Buy milk',\n  completed: false,\n})\nawait transaction.when('settled')\n```\n\nThis Collection has no `sync` option. Its normal insert, update, and delete handlers save local mutations to SQLite. It does not contact a server. The browser must support OPFS and run in a secure context. If multiple tabs can open the same database, use the [multi-tab setup](#browser-tabs-and-electron-renderers).\n\n### Manual transactions on a local Collection\n\nFor a Collection without a `sync` option, normal Collection mutations persist automatically. A manual transaction must call `acceptMutations` in its mutation function:\n\n```ts\nimport { createTransaction } from '@tanstack/db'\n\nconst manualTransaction = createTransaction({\n  autoCommit: false,\n  mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    await todos.utils.acceptMutations(transaction)\n  },\n})\n\nmanualTransaction.mutate(() => {\n  todos.insert({ id: '2', title: 'Call home', completed: false })\n})\n\nawait manualTransaction.commit()\n```\n\n### Load rows after reopening the database\n\nKeep the database open while its Collections run. To simulate a page restart, clean up the Collection and close the database:\n\n```ts\nawait todos.cleanup()\nawait database.close?.()\n```\n\nOpen the same OPFS database and create a Collection with the same `id` and `schemaVersion`. Load its rows before you read them:\n\n```ts\nconst reopenedDatabase = await openBrowserWASQLiteOPFSDatabase({\n  databaseName: 'app.sqlite',\n})\nconst reopenedPersistence = createBrowserWASQLitePersistence({\n  database: reopenedDatabase,\n})\nconst reopenedTodos = createCollection(\n  persistedCollectionOptions<Todo, string>({\n    id: 'todos',\n    getKey: (todo) => todo.id,\n    persistence: reopenedPersistence,\n    schemaVersion: 1,\n  }),\n)\n\nawait reopenedTodos.preload()\nconsole.log(reopenedTodos.get('1')?.title) // 'Buy milk'\n\nawait reopenedTodos.cleanup()\nawait reopenedDatabase.close?.()\n```\n\n## Temporal values\n\nSQLite persistence preserves `Temporal.Instant` and `Temporal.PlainDate` as\nnative values, including nested fields, metadata and replay. Register the same\nTemporal implementation globally before writing or reopening persisted data.\nOn a runtime without native Temporal, for example:\n\n```ts\nimport 'temporal-polyfill/global'\n```\n\nMissing constructors and other Temporal kinds reject explicitly. Strings remain\nstrings. Equality retains the Temporal kind and canonical text, including a\nPlainDate's calendar; ordering follows Temporal comparison. Supported native\npredicates and persisted expression indexes use the existing SQL filtering and\nin-memory cleanup pipeline without reducing nanosecond precision.\n\nThis support applies to the shared SQLite adapter and local persisted wrapper.\nThe remote-subset coordinator wire domain does not include Temporal literals;\nthose requests reject before remote retry admission. If retained local demand\nbecomes remote after an ownership change, fallback sequence-gap recovery fails\nthe current Collection sync run before retrying that invalid request. This is a\nterminal Collection error: later active subsets are not recovered, subsequent\nloads reject with the same error, and later coordinator messages do not resume\nthe failed run. Existing acquisitions can still be released. Multiprocess\ntransport of native Temporal rows is not covered by this support claim.\n\nValues already stored as `{}` cannot be recovered. If an older version damaged\na synced cache, replace it through the existing `schemaVersion` and schema-reset\npolicy, then refetch. Updating the library does not automatically erase data.\nAlso rebuild an older cache if ordinary objects used the reserved\n`__tanstack_db_persisted_type__` marker with a newly recognized type name. Older\nwrites did not escape those objects, so their original meaning is ambiguous.\nNew writes escape the marker and preserve the ordinary object.\nOlder library versions cannot interpret the new Temporal encoding. Changed\nexpression indexes rebuild when first ensured after upgrade. Later startups reuse\nthose indexes. A changed native-literal or escaped-record signature can leave an\nobsolete registry entry and index until an explicit schema reset; this release\ndoes not reclaim those entries automatically. Increment the affected synced\nCollection's `schemaVersion` and retain its reset policy to rebuild that cache.\nThe reset removes its old values and indexes before reading the new schema;\nother Collections with unchanged schema versions retain their caches. Refetch\nfrom the upstream source after reset.\n\n## Add persistence to a synced Collection\n\nPass the options from a sync adapter into `persistedCollectionOptions`. The wrapper keeps that adapter's sync behavior and stores its applied data in SQLite.\n\nFor example, you can wrap a Query Collection. This separate example uses the imports and `Todo` type from the browser example above:\n\n```ts\nimport { QueryClient } from '@tanstack/query-core'\nimport { queryCollectionOptions } from '@tanstack/query-db-collection'\n\nconst syncedDatabase = await openBrowserWASQLiteOPFSDatabase({\n  databaseName: 'synced.sqlite',\n})\nconst syncedPersistence = createBrowserWASQLitePersistence({\n  database: syncedDatabase,\n})\nconst queryClient = new QueryClient()\n\nconst syncedTodos = createCollection(\n  persistedCollectionOptions<Todo, string>({\n    ...queryCollectionOptions<Todo, string>({\n      id: 'synced-todos',\n      queryClient,\n      queryKey: ['todos'],\n      queryFn: async () => {\n        const response = await fetch('http://localhost:3000/api/todos')\n        if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Could not load todos')\n        return (await response.json()) as Array<Todo>\n      },\n      getKey: (todo) => todo.id,\n    }),\n    persistence: syncedPersistence,\n    schemaVersion: 1,\n    initialRender: {\n      strategy: 'network-first',\n      networkTimeoutMs: 3_000,\n    },\n  }),\n)\n```\n\nInstall `@tanstack/query-core` and `@tanstack/query-db-collection` to use this example. Change the URL to your server. You can wrap another Collection sync adapter in the same way.\n\nThe `initialRender` option is optional. It lets live queries report when SQLite restore completes. It also lets React and Solid Suspense use restored rows for the first render while the Collection is not ready.\n\nKeep `syncedDatabase` open while `syncedTodos` runs. During normal shutdown, await `syncedTodos.cleanup()` and then `syncedDatabase.close?.()`.\n\nThe `sync` option determines which mode the wrapper uses:\n\n| Collection options | SQLite behavior |\n| --- | --- |\n| `sync` present | Load persisted rows, then apply source sync transactions. The source remains responsible for current data. |\n| `sync` absent | Load and save local rows through a loopback sync adapter. The Collection has no remote source. |\n\nThe wrapper also supports `syncMode: 'on-demand'`. In that mode, it loads rows for active query demand rather than loading every stored row into the Collection.\n\n## Render after SQLite restore\n\n`initialRender` requires an eager persisted Collection. It does not support `syncMode: 'on-demand'`, which has no complete startup snapshot. Set it on every source Collection in a live query. If one source does not opt in, the query cannot report persisted readiness or use the restored snapshot as its initial-render fallback.\n\nInstall the React binding to use the following components:\n\n```sh\nnpm install @tanstack/react-db\n```\n\nA regular React live query exposes both readiness signals:\n\n```tsx\nimport { useLiveQuery } from '@tanstack/react-db'\n\nfunction TodosView() {\n  const result = useLiveQuery((q) => q.from({ syncedTodos }))\n  const readiness = result.isReady\n    ? 'Collection ready'\n    : result.isPersistedReady\n      ? 'SQLite restore complete. Collection readiness pending'\n      : 'Waiting for Collection or SQLite restore'\n\n  return (\n    <>\n      <p>{readiness}</p>\n      <ul>{result.data.map((todo) => <li key={todo.id}>{todo.title}</li>)}</ul>\n    </>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n`isReady` reports Collection readiness. `isPersistedReady` reports that every opted-in source finished its current eager restore, even when SQLite had no rows. These flags do not identify where each visible row came from. A regular `useLiveQuery` does not wait for the network-first deadline before it renders rows.\n\nThe hooks also expose `persistedStatus` and `persistedError`. The status is `unavailable` if a source did not opt in, `loading` during restore, `ready` after every restore completes, or `error` if a restore fails. `persistedError` keeps the restore error. An SSR seed does not count as a SQLite restore.\n\nReact, Solid, and Svelte read these values as properties. Angular reads signals such as `result.isPersistedReady()`. Vue reads refs such as `result.isPersistedReady.value`.\n\nReact and Solid Suspense can use the same option for network-first initial rendering. This React component uses the `syncedTodos` Collection configured above:\n\n```tsx\nimport { Suspense } from 'react'\nimport { useLiveSuspenseQuery } from '@tanstack/react-db'\n\nfunction TodosList() {\n  const { data } = useLiveSuspenseQuery((q) => q.from({ syncedTodos }))\n  return <ul>{data.map((todo) => <li key={todo.id}>{todo.title}</li>)}</ul>\n}\n\nfunction TodosScreen() {\n  return (\n    <Suspense fallback={<p>Loading todos...</p>}>\n      <TodosList />\n    </Suspense>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nPlace `TodosScreen` inside an [Error Boundary](./error-handling.md#using-suspense-and-error-boundaries-react) to handle initial failures.\n\nSuspense prefers Collection readiness. If it remains pending after `networkTimeoutMs`, a completed SQLite restore can release the first render. The default is 3 seconds. Set `networkTimeoutMs: 0` to allow fallback as soon as restore completes. For a query with multiple sources, the longest configured deadline applies. The deadline does not stop network sync.\n\nA source failure can allow fallback before the deadline. React also waits for restore if its client query stream fails before the first render, including before the component mounts. Once every restore succeeds, React renders the derived query result, even if it is empty.\n\nA derived-query failure still reaches the surrounding React Error Boundary. If the network-first wait rejects, its error reaches that boundary. A successful network load can render even if restore failed or a source did not opt in.\n\nPersisted readiness does not prove current authorization. Isolate SQLite data by user or tenant. If an authorization failure must hide cached rows, block the view separately. The fallback does not classify source errors.\n\n## Browser tabs and Electron renderers\n\nThe browser package uses single-tab coordination by default. If tabs share one OPFS database, give the persistence instance a `BrowserCollectionCoordinator`. It elects an owner for each Collection and routes writes between tabs. Replace the initial database and persistence setup with this code in each tab:\n\n```ts\nimport { BrowserCollectionCoordinator } from '@tanstack/browser-db-sqlite-persistence'\n\nconst database = await openBrowserWASQLiteOPFSDatabase({\n  databaseName: 'app.sqlite',\n})\nconst coordinator = new BrowserCollectionCoordinator({ dbName: 'app' })\nconst persistence = createBrowserWASQLitePersistence({\n  database,\n  coordinator,\n})\n```\n\nPass this persistence instance to `persistedCollectionOptions`. During normal shutdown, clean up the Collections, dispose the coordinator, and close the database.\n\nThe Electron package uses a main-process SQLite owner and a renderer bridge. Add `ElectronCollectionCoordinator` when multiple renderers share the database.\n\nThese coordinators belong to SQLite persistence. The offline transaction executor has separate leader election for its outbox. Configure each system for the storage it owns.\n\nThe browser database uses a worker. Its OPFS setup needs a secure context and browser support for OPFS. Close the database during normal shutdown. After a page returns from the back/forward cache, create fresh database and Collection instances.\n\n## Node.js alternative\n\nFor a Node.js process, install `@tanstack/node-db-sqlite-persistence` and `better-sqlite3` in place of the browser packages. Open a file with `better-sqlite3` and pass it to the Node factory:\n\n```ts\nimport Database from 'better-sqlite3'\nimport {\n  createNodeSQLitePersistence,\n  persistedCollectionOptions,\n} from '@tanstack/node-db-sqlite-persistence'\n\nconst nodeDatabase = new Database('./app.sqlite')\nconst nodePersistence = createNodeSQLitePersistence({\n  database: nodeDatabase,\n})\n```\n\nUse `nodePersistence` in the Collection options shown above. Clean up each Collection before you call `nodeDatabase.close()`. The [Node package README](https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/node-db-sqlite-persistence#readme) has a complete example.\n\n## Schema versions and recovery\n\nBy default, a synced Collection resets its persisted data after a schema mismatch. Its sync adapter must then load current data. A Collection without `sync` reports an error instead. It has no source that can restore deleted local rows.\n\nAll runtime factories listed above except the Electron renderer factory accept `schemaMismatchPolicy`. An explicit `reset` policy can delete local data, so use it only when your application can restore that data.\n\nThe Node package prunes its applied transaction log by default. Its [README](https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/packages/node-db-sqlite-persistence#applied-transaction-pruning) lists the limits and options. If a resume position is older than the retained log, persistence loads a full snapshot.\n\n## Errors and limits\n\nAn unavailable browser storage capability raises `PersistenceUnavailableError`. A durable write failure raises `PersistedCollectionDurabilityError` and puts the Collection in its error state.\n\nIf a coordinated write loses its response, the result can be uncertain. `IndeterminateCommitError` means the application must check the durable state before it retries that write.\n\nPersistence retains source data and metadata. It does not define how an application sends local changes to a server. Use a mutation handler or the [offline transactions guide](./offline-transactions.md) for that work.\n"}
